# Mutation plan for the five slash-command writes — createCommand, updateCommand,
# deleteCommand, moveCommand and regenCommandToken.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/command-writes.plan
#
# The decisions a reader could plausibly get wrong, none of which a status code alone would catch:
#
#   * the built-in trigger registry, which this port replaces with 33 strings. Drop one and a
#     client can register a command that shadows a built-in; reserve one of the two switched-off
#     providers and a legal trigger is refused.
#   * `excludeCommandID`, in both polarities — it is the only thing that lets a command keep its
#     own trigger across an update, and dropping it makes every update a duplicate.
#   * which team `MoveCommand` checks the trigger against: the destination, not the source.
#   * the nine fields `UpdateCommand` copies off the old command, four of which a client supplies
#     in the same request body and none of which may survive it.
#   * where `UpdateAt` is stamped — in the *store*, which is the only reason a move or a token
#     regeneration bumps it at all.
#   * the ownership ladder's two rungs, 404 then 403, and `moveCommand`'s destination gate, which
#     answers 403 where its siblings answer 404.
#   * `go_json_marshal` versus `serde_json::to_string`, which differ only on `&`, `<` and `>`.
#   * `ReturnStatusOK`'s exact bytes, and `regenCommandToken`'s missing trailing newline.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

